"""Progressively disclosed career guidance; facts and community reports stay distinct."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
from html import escape as e
DATA=json.loads((Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent/'data/career-guidance.json').read_text())

def disclosure_control():
 return '<span class="disclosure-control" aria-hidden="true"><span class="disclosure-plus">+</span><span class="disclosure-minus">−</span></span>'
def drawer(b,title,lead,body,ident=''):
 return '<details class="career-guide-drawer"'+(' id="'+ident+'"' if ident else '')+'><summary><span class="drawer-label"><strong>'+title+'</strong><small class="drawer-preview">'+lead+'</small><span class="drawer-action" aria-hidden="true"><span class="drawer-closed-label">Read answer</span><span class="drawer-open-label">Close answer</span></span></span>'+disclosure_control()+'</summary><div class="career-guide-body">'+body+'</div></details>'
def paras(rows):
 return '<dl class="guidance-points">'+''.join('<div><dt>'+label+'</dt><dd>'+copy+'</dd></div>' for label,copy in rows)+'</dl>'
def refs(b,items):
 return '<nav class="career-inline-sources" aria-label="Sources for this guidance">'+''.join(b.link('#source-'+sid,label) for sid,label in items)+'</nav>'
def panel(b,ident,title,lead,body):
 return '<section class="tool-section career-panel" id="'+ident+'" data-career-panel><header class="career-panel-heading"><h2>'+title+'</h2><p>'+lead+'</p><span class="panel-expand-hint">Tap a question to open its answer</span></header>'+body+'</section>'
def route_depth(b,ident):
 d=DATA['routes'][ident]
 return drawer(b,'Skills, progression & questions','Look beyond the job title.',paras([('Build these skills.',e(d['skills'])),('Where it can lead.',e(d['progression'])),('Ask before committing.',e(d['questions'])),('Build evidence of your skills.',e(d['evidence']))]))
def panels(b):
 study=drawer(b,'Work first or study further?','Choose the training for the work you want.',paras([
 ('Start with a supervised job if…','you need income, want to test a work setting or need clinical confidence. Compare the mentor and induction plan, not just the hospital name. A job can clarify a later postgraduate choice; keep an eye on admission cycles. Ask for a first-month learning plan: shared shifts, types of cases you will initially handle and when records are reviewed. If you are unsure about a clinical MVSc, a supported job can reveal whether you enjoy the actual work. Avoid using a poorly supported first job as proof that the entire profession is wrong for you.'),
 ('Consider MVSc if…','a discipline genuinely interests you or the roles you want require relevant postgraduate training. Clinical subjects, diagnostics, production and public health lead to different daily work. An MVSc is not a guarantee of a faculty post or a recognised specialist title. Before applying, write one sentence: “I want this discipline because I want to become better at ___.” Then find departments where students actually do that work. If the only answer is that a seat is available or everyone is applying, investigate the training and alternatives before committing. The supervisor and access to cases or methods can matter more than the subject label.'),
 ('Consider a PhD if…','you enjoy forming questions, analysing evidence and writing—not only learning facts. Check the actual research or teaching posts you hope to pursue, then assess the supervisor, project, funding and facilities. Try reading a paper and proposing the next experiment, including what might go wrong. If that process interests you, ask potential supervisors about project feasibility and training. If you mainly want clinical confidence, a research-heavy programme may not provide the supervised caseload you need. Check whether your intended next job requires a doctorate rather than treating it as an automatic next step.'),
 ('MPH, management or data training?','Start from vacancies that accept your qualification. Public-health, business and technology courses vary in eligibility and outcomes. Ask whether the course teaches a missing skill; an additional degree alone does not qualify you for every related job. Collect several current vacancies and mark the repeated requirements. For an MPH, look for methods training and a real applied project; for management, examine internships and role-specific placement outcomes; for data work, inspect the portfolio graduates can produce. Compare this with learning the missing skill while working. Do not take on a large fee based only on a course’s broad career list.')]))
 study+=drawer(b,'Which postgraduate discipline fits?','Match the work, then investigate the department.',paras([
 ('Patients & procedures','Explore medicine, surgery, gynaecology or other relevant clinical disciplines. Ask about supervised caseload, procedures, referral work and how responsibilities increase. Ask a current student what they personally did last week, what they only observed and how feedback was given. A busy hospital can still offer little hands-on teaching. Find out who supervises out-of-hours cases, how procedural responsibility increases and whether case records are discussed. Prefer specific examples over a claim of “excellent clinical exposure”.'),
 ('Samples & mechanisms','Explore pathology, microbiology, parasitology, pharmacology or related research areas. Look for functioning equipment, biosafety, sample access and training in interpreting results. A useful question is: “Which methods could I perform and explain independently by graduation?” Check whether consumables, working equipment and trained supervisors are available. Ask how often instruments are down and whether your project depends on one outside laboratory. The thesis title can sound advanced while the actual training remains narrow.'),
 ('Herds, food & populations','Explore nutrition, genetics, production, livestock products, epidemiology or public health. Check the field projects, datasets, industry links and the work graduates actually enter. Ask to see examples of recent student projects and where graduates work. A production course can involve farm management, laboratory analysis or statistical modelling; those lead to different skills. Check whether you will have access to usable records, field visits and someone who can teach the required analysis. A company contact alone is not a guaranteed internship.'),
 ('Foundations & teaching','Anatomy, physiology and biochemistry can lead towards teaching and research. Ask what methods you will learn and check current appointment requirements before assuming a teaching route. Look beyond the examination syllabus: will you learn microscopy, experimental methods, physiology instrumentation, data analysis or teaching design? Ask which skills have helped recent graduates obtain their next position. If teaching is the goal, read current appointment notices now, including discipline and qualification requirements, rather than discovering them after finishing the degree.')]))
 study+=drawer(b,'Admissions: follow the right route','An exam application and a college application can be separate.',
 '<ol><li><strong>Pick the programme and institution.</strong> Read its current prospectus, recognised qualifications, subject eligibility and registration or internship requirements.</li><li><strong>Identify the exact selection route.</strong> Check whether the applicable veterinary seats use an ICAR-linked process, a university examination or another route. Do not assume one exam covers every MVSc seat.</li><li><strong>Track every stage.</strong> Examination, institutional application, counselling, document verification and fee deadlines may be separate.</li><li><strong>Prepare the documents early.</strong> Check degree/provisional certificates, transcripts, internship completion, registration and category documents against the current notice.</li></ol>'+refs(b,[('pg-nta','NTA: current ICAR notices'),('ivri','IVRI: MVSc information'),('pg-tanuvas','TANUVAS: PG admissions')]))
 study+=drawer(b,'Before accepting a seat','Talk to current students as well as the department.',paras([
 ('Learning quality','Who supervises you? How often do you meet? Is the promised clinical exposure or laboratory facility actually available to students? Request one concrete example of a student learning a difficult method: who taught it, how much practice they received and how competence was assessed. Ask current students whether meetings actually happen and whether feedback is useful. A famous guide who has no time for you may offer a different experience from the one you imagined.'),
 ('Money & time','Get written information on fees, stipend availability, accommodation, bonds and conditions. Include living costs and income you would forgo; do not budget from an old social-media post. Write a monthly budget with tuition, rent, food, travel and existing obligations. Keep an unconfirmed stipend out of the guaranteed-income column. Ask when payments normally arrive and what happens during delays. Compare the total commitment with a realistic job option; the relevant cost includes both money spent and earnings you give up.'),
 ('Outcomes','Ask what recent graduates do and how the programme helped them get there. Speak to more than one student; one excellent or unhappy experience is not the whole department. Ask for recent examples from your chosen department, not a university-wide success story. Which students entered jobs, further study or unrelated work? How did they find those opportunities? A department that can connect you with alumni gives you something to verify. Outcomes are context, not a promise that your result will be identical.'),
 ('A useful final question','If this degree did not bring an immediate job offer, would the training still move you towards work you want to do? Make the answer specific: supervised case management, a method you can demonstrate, better analytical writing or preparation for a defined role. If you cannot name the skill and explain how the programme teaches it, gather more information. Do not confuse a recognisable degree title with a clear training plan.')] ))
 study+=drawer(b,'Compare two departments fairly','Ask for evidence behind each promise.',paras([
 ('Supervision & access','Ask who will teach the methods you want to learn, how often you meet and whether students can use the advertised facilities. Speak to current students in that discipline. Put the same questions to both departments. Ask to see a normal timetable, the process for allocating cases or equipment and an example of feedback. If one answer is vague, mark it as unknown rather than awarding it points for reputation. Speak with students at different stages; induction and final-year experiences can differ.'),
 ('Project feasibility','Check whether the proposed work has the samples, approvals, equipment, data and funding it needs. Ask what happens when a key resource is unavailable. Ask what must happen before data collection can begin and who is responsible for each dependency. A project needing several permissions or an uncertain sample supply needs a fallback. Check that the fallback still teaches worthwhile methods. Clarify authorship and data access early through the institution’s processes, not after disagreements arise.'),
 ('Training you can demonstrate','List the procedures, analytical methods, writing or teaching experience you expect to leave with. Ask how feedback and assessment make those skills visible. Turn “good exposure” into outputs: a reviewed case log, a validated laboratory skill, a reproducible analysis, a teaching session or a well-written thesis chapter. Ask who assesses these and how often. You should be able to explain your own contribution at a future interview without relying on the department’s name.'),
 ('Total commitment','Compare tuition, living costs, confirmed funding, bond terms and realistic completion expectations. Record unknown costs instead of assuming a stipend will cover them. Compare both options on one sheet: confirmed funding, unavoidable costs, learning access, supervision and the next role each could support. Record the source of each answer. If the choice turns on an assumption, such as an expected stipend or placement, verify that assumption before accepting rather than hoping it works out.')]))
 out=panel(b,'pg-or-practice','Further study, with a purpose','Job, MVSc or another degree? Start with the work you want to learn.',study)
 countries=[
 ('United Kingdom','RCVS recognition → registration','uk',[
 ('1 · Check recognition','Use the RCVS guidance for your exact primary qualification. Do not assume that an Indian BVSc or a UK master’s automatically permits clinical practice. Check the exact degree-awarding institution and graduation circumstances, not just the words “BVSc”. Save the regulator’s answer or applicable guidance with the date. Research admission and permission to practise are separate decisions; an offer for a taught master’s does not settle registration eligibility.'),
 ('2 · Follow the applicable route','If your qualification is not recognised, check eligibility for the statutory membership examination, including documents, English requirements and the current written and clinical assessment process. Before paying for preparation, map the required stages and document deadlines from the current RCVS guidance. Assess your clinical skills as well as written-exam knowledge. Ask a supervisor which areas need practice; knowing the theory and demonstrating a skill under assessment are different tasks.'),
 ('3 · Plan beyond the exam','Complete the applicable registration requirements, then separately check immigration permission and employment. Ask a prospective employer about induction and support. Build two parallel checklists: professional registration and the right to live and work there. Do not rely on a course agent to answer both. Once considering a job, ask about a gradual induction, the types of cases you will see and how clinical support works while you learn a different practice system.')],'rcvs','RCVS: recognition and statutory exam'),
 ('United States','Equivalency + NAVLE + state requirements','us',[
 ('1 · Choose a jurisdiction','Check the state licensing board before paying for an equivalency pathway. Acceptance of ECFVG or PAVE and additional requirements must be verified for that jurisdiction. Get the board’s requirements for your exact qualification and intended route. If you later change states, recheck them before assuming your previous plan transfers unchanged. When comparing ECFVG and PAVE, compare the whole pathway and required clinical components, not just the first examination fee.'),
 ('2 · Map the assessments','Graduates of non-accredited schools may need an educational equivalency pathway. Check the applicable BCSE or QSE milestone for NAVLE eligibility, plus the remaining clinical and credential requirements. BCSE is the relevant ECFVG milestone and QSE the PAVE milestone in ICVA’s NAVLE eligibility guidance; they are not two interchangeable names for the same exam. Confirm your board’s approval requirements. Maintain a checklist separating educational equivalency, NAVLE and the remaining licensing steps so passing one milestone does not create a false sense of completion.'),
 ('3 · Finish the licensing route','Passing NAVLE alone is not a licence. Check the state’s application, remaining examinations or documentation, and immigration/work authorisation separately. Ask the board what still remains for you personally before accepting clinical duties. Budget for the period between assessment and employability, and verify permitted work while waiting. A research or non-clinical role may have a different eligibility route, but it does not itself authorise independent veterinary practice.')],'icva','ICVA: NAVLE application and eligibility'),
 ('Canada','NEB certification → provincial registration','canada',[
 ('1 · Start with the NEB','Check how your qualification is assessed and which examinations or recognised equivalencies apply. The Canadian process should not be treated as identical to a US state licence. If Canadian licensure is your goal, ICVA directs NAVLE applicants to the Canadian NEB process. Choose the intended province too, so you can read its registration requirements alongside the national examination pathway. Do not build the plan solely from a friend’s US experience.'),
 ('2 · Understand the sequence','For the applicable non-accredited-graduate pathway, review BCSE, NAVLE and the clinical assessment requirements, including PSA/CPE where required. Use the current NEB rules for ordering and eligibility. The NEB overview states that non-accredited graduates must pass BCSE before NAVLE or CPE eligibility. Check the separate clinical-exam guidance for your remaining prerequisites and waiting-list process. Avoid non-refundable travel before receiving the appropriate confirmation. Examination availability can shape your budget as much as the published fee.'),
 ('3 · Check the province','The NEB Certificate of Qualification and registration with the intended provincial regulator are distinct stages. Language, local registration and immigration requirements also need checking. Keep certification, provincial registration and work permission as separate rows in your plan. Ask which documents must come directly from your university or regulator and how current they must be. An employer’s interest is useful, but it is not a substitute for confirmation that you can legally perform the advertised duties.')],'canada-neb','CVMA/NEB: examinations and certification'),
 ('Australia & New Zealand','Recognised qualification or AVE route','anz',[
 ('1 · Check your qualification','Use AVBC’s recognition guidance. If your qualification is not recognised, establish eligibility for the Australasian Veterinary Examination before planning a move. AVBC describes three AVE stages: eligibility assessment, preliminary examination and clinical examination. Begin at eligibility, including the relevant language and document requirements, rather than buying preparation for the final assessment first. Use the official recognition information for your qualification.'),
 ('2 · Read the current handbook','Check the preliminary and clinical examination pathway, language and document requirements, available sittings and applicable fees. Budget for the route you actually need. List the total costs and dependencies for each stage, including travel and time away from paid work. If you need practical preparation, ask what supervised opportunities are available and permitted. Avoid any provider claiming that attendance at its course guarantees an examination result or registration.'),
 ('3 · Register where you will work','Examination success and registration are separate. Check the relevant Australian state or territory board, or the Veterinary Council of New Zealand, plus immigration and employment requirements. AVE completion allows you to apply to the relevant registration authority; it is not the same as already being on its register. Migration skills assessment, immigration permission and employment also need separate attention. Ask a prospective employer about induction and local professional expectations after registration.')],'avbc','AVBC: Australasian Veterinary Examination')]
 abroad='<p class="career-key-note"><strong>A master’s abroad is not a clinical licence.</strong> Research, study, registration, employment and visas each have their own rules.</p>'
 for title,lead,ident,rows,sid,label in countries:
  abroad+=drawer(b,title,lead,paras(rows)+refs(b,[(sid,label)]),'country-'+ident)
 abroad+=drawer(b,'Can I afford the route?','Build a budget before choosing a country.',paras([
 ('Price the full journey','List credential verification, language tests, each assessment, preparation, clinical placements where required, travel, accommodation, registration and visa costs using current official figures. Use a sheet with stage, official fee, currency, due date, travel and living costs. Add the date you checked each figure. Do not compare countries using only the first exam fee. A route with a cheaper first step can still require expensive clinical assessment, relocation or a longer period without your expected income.'),
 ('Plan for delays','Include living costs, limited earning opportunities while preparing and a retake contingency. A friend’s timeline or an agent’s promise is not a regulator’s guarantee. Run a second budget in which an exam or placement is delayed and one assessment must be repeated. Include fixed commitments at home. Decide which costs you can absorb without unsafe borrowing. This is a planning scenario, not a prediction that you will fail; it prevents a delay from forcing a rushed financial decision.'),
 ('Keep a second option','Decide what you will do while waiting: supervised work, relevant research or another realistic local role. Set a spending limit and a date to review progress. Make it concrete: continue a supervised job until a stated milestone, build savings to a defined amount, or postpone relocation until eligibility is confirmed. Set a review date and a maximum affordable spend. Changing the timeline after learning the real requirements is sensible planning, not a personal failure.'),
 ('Verify before paying','Use the regulator’s current handbook and fee schedule. Check any course or agent’s claims directly; no preparation provider can guarantee a licence or job. Ask the regulator whether a claimed route is accepted for your circumstances. Ask the provider for written fees, refund rules and exactly what it supplies. Testimonials show individual experiences, not your eligibility. Keep original records yourself rather than making an intermediary your only route to essential documents.')]))
 abroad+=drawer(b,'Build your document & cost tracker','One checklist for the country you choose.',paras([
 ('Documents','Check the regulator’s current list for degree and transcripts, registration or good-standing evidence, identity documents, English evidence and any certified translations. Note who must send each document and its validity period. Create columns for issuer, delivery method, processing time, expiry and status. Start early with records that must be sent directly by an institution. Check name consistency across identity and academic documents. Keep secure copies and correspondence; do not send originals to an unofficial agent merely because they promise a faster route.'),
 ('Cost ledger','Create a row for every required stage: official fee, currency, payment deadline, travel, living costs and retake contingency. Mark estimates separately from confirmed quotations. Add a base scenario and a delayed scenario, and identify which payments are refundable. Record exchange-rate assumptions without treating them as fixed. Include local obligations and any period when you cannot earn as a registered veterinarian. Review the total before each large payment rather than following a sunk-cost feeling that you have already spent too much to stop.'),
 ('Eligibility before bookings','Confirm that you qualify for the next assessment before paying for preparation or travel. Exam eligibility, a job offer and permission to work are separate checks. Put the approving authority and confirmation date beside each stage. If you are unsure whether a previous examination or qualification counts, obtain clarification first. A preparation course accepting your fee is not confirmation that the regulator will accept your application. Treat those as separate decisions.'),
 ('Employer questions','Ask which duties you can legally perform before registration, who provides supervision, and whether any promised sponsorship or reimbursement is in writing. Verify the answer with the regulator or immigration authority. Ask what happens if registration or a visa takes longer than expected: start date, duties, pay, accommodation and repayment clauses may change. Read any reimbursement bond carefully. If the proposed role includes restricted duties, obtain clear guidance from the relevant regulator instead of assuming that supervision makes every activity permissible.')]))
 out+=panel(b,'overseas','Work abroad: know the route','Choose a destination to see the steps and what needs checking.',abroad)
 toolkit=drawer(b,'Make an application that shows your strengths','A concise CV, a work sample and a vacancy tracker.',paras([
 ('Tailor the first page','Match your qualifications and actual experience to the essential criteria. Describe your contribution honestly: observed, assisted and independently performed are different levels of responsibility. Use the advertisement’s essential criteria as a checklist. Put the relevant species, methods, placements or outputs near the top, and explain your level of responsibility. Replace “hard-working team player” with a short example of a task completed with a team. Keep claims consistent with what a supervisor could verify.'),
 ('Show one relevant example','Use an anonymised, authorised case discussion, evidence brief, research summary or technical explainer. Explain what you did, what you learned and who supervised it. A clinic application might use a case reflection; a laboratory role a quality-control example; an industry role a short training presentation. State the context, your contribution and the limitation. Do not send a confidential case file or a large generic portfolio that makes the employer search for the relevant piece.'),
 ('Track the process','Record the official vacancy URL, deadline, required documents, contact, application date and follow-up. Keep a copy of the advertisement and your submitted application. Search official institutional and employer pages alongside professional networks. Ask seniors for a specific introduction rather than a vague request to find you any job. Follow up politely through the stated channel and keep the message brief. Record rejections and unanswered applications so you can improve the approach without repeatedly applying blindly.'),
 ('Check the offer','Confirm the employer, duties, pay, supervision, contract and location through reliable contact channels. Compare the actual opportunity with your priorities before accepting. Verify the organisation and speak with someone doing the job. Understand probation, pay timing, travel reimbursement, duty hours and exit conditions. A request for unusual recruitment payments or pressure to hand over original documents deserves independent verification. Do not resign or relocate based only on an informal message.')]))
 toolkit+=drawer(b,'Your decision worksheet','Fill it in, then copy it for your own notes.',
 '<p>Use two real opportunities. Your answers stay in this page; they are not submitted or saved.</p><div class="career-worksheet"><label for="career-priorities">My three priorities<textarea id="career-priorities" rows="2" placeholder="For example: near home, named mentor, sustainable hours"></textarea></label><label for="career-options">The two options and what I still need to check<textarea id="career-options" rows="3" placeholder="Option A… Option B… Unknowns…"></textarea></label><label for="career-next-step">My next action and review date<textarea id="career-next-step" rows="2" placeholder="Who will I speak to, what will I try, and when will I review it?"></textarea></label><button type="button" class="js-only" data-copy-career-plan>Copy my worksheet</button><p role="status" data-plan-status></p></div>')
 decide='<span id="next-30-days" class="anchor-alias"></span><p class="career-key-note"><strong>Choose the next useful step, not your whole life.</strong> Compare two realistic opportunities and find out what is still unknown.</p>'
 decide+=drawer(b,'1. Set your non-negotiables','Location, finances, hours and support.',paras([
 ('What must be true?','Write down your minimum income needs, acceptable locations, family responsibilities and how long you can fund study or exam preparation. Separate a requirement from a preference. Put a number or boundary beside each requirement: the amount needed for living costs, the locations you can accept, or the hours needed for caring responsibilities. Then list preferences separately. This prevents an impressive job title from winning a comparison even though the actual offer cannot work for your life.'),
 ('Which working day suits you?','Think about consultations, procedures, farm travel, laboratory work, data, writing or teaching. Consider nights, emergencies and public-facing work—not only your favourite subject. Think about a Tuesday, not a graduation photo. Would you rather explain estimates to clients, travel between farms, review samples, teach a class or work through a dataset? You do not have to love every task. You need enough interest in the recurring work and a realistic way to manage the parts you find difficult.'),
 ('What support do you need now?','A first role should match your competence. Identify the supervision, equipment and escalation support needed to work safely; a prestigious title does not replace them. List the situations in which you currently need a senior, then ask how the role handles them. Needing supervision after graduation is normal; being promised support that never appears is a different problem. Choose a setting where responsibility increases with demonstrated competence and help is actually accessible.')]))
 decide+=drawer(b,'2. Compare opportunities, not stereotypes','“Government” or “private” is not a job description.',paras([
 ('Use the same questions','For each option record daily work, eligibility, actual location, take-home pay, duty hours, mentorship and the next learning opportunity. Mark unknown answers as unknown rather than scoring them optimistically. Compare two real offers or departments, not an ideal government job against the worst private clinic you have heard about. First eliminate options that fail your non-negotiables. Then choose a few priorities, such as supervision, location and financial sustainability. Do not let a high score on prestige compensate for missing safe clinical support.'),
 ('Read the written terms','Check allowances, accommodation, probation, notice, bond conditions and contract duration. Government vacancies also have specific posting and selection rules; do not assume a post will open when you need it. Work out usable income after unavoidable housing, commuting and job-related expenses. Include duty hours and on-call expectations when judging whether the offer is workable. Ask who pays for training and what repayment or notice conditions apply. If a verbal promise matters to your decision, request it in the written offer.'),
 ('Choose your trade-off','Decide what matters most right now. A better mentor may outweigh a modest pay difference; family location may outweigh a preferred sector. There is no universal best order. Write: “For the next stage I am choosing ___ because ___; I will review this when ___.” For example, accepting a less convenient location for a clearly supervised role is different from accepting it for a vague promise of exposure. Name what you gain, what you give up and what evidence would justify changing direction.'),
 ('Ask about a normal week','Speak to a recent graduate and someone established in the exact kind of role. Ask what takes most of their time, what is difficult and what they wish they had checked before joining. Ask the junior colleague what happened on their last difficult shift, who helped and whether they could take leave. Ask the senior how they train new staff and review mistakes. Look for agreement between those accounts. A general statement that the team is friendly is less informative than a concrete example of support.')]))
 decide+=drawer(b,'3. Try a small version of the work','Replace guessing with an experience you can reflect on.',paras([
 ('Arrange a safe trial','Through your college, request an approved observation visit, supervised placement or small project. Do not independently perform clinical work beyond your training or registration. Agree the purpose, supervision, hours and permitted activities before attending. A short observation visit can answer whether the environment suits you; it should not become open-ended unpaid clinical cover. If access is not possible, arrange structured conversations with people in the role and ask for a realistic description of their tasks.'),
 ('Notice what energises you','After the experience, record what you enjoyed, what drained you, what you could improve with practice and which conditions would need to change. Separate the work from the workplace. Disliking an unsupported night shift does not necessarily mean you dislike clinical medicine. Enjoying a welcoming department does not prove you enjoy its research methods. Record one example of each: task you liked, task you disliked, missing skill and condition that affected the experience.'),
 ('Make one reversible commitment','Apply for a suitable role, visit a department, learn one relevant method or start a defined exam-preparation block. Set a review date and decide what evidence would make you change direction. Decide what you need to learn next, who can help and when you will reassess. Examples include a supervised role with a defined induction, a department visit before counselling, or a focused preparation block alongside work. Judge progress by evidence gathered and skills built, not by whether your choice looks permanent to other people.')]))
 decide+=drawer(b,'4. Prepare to apply & decide','Questions, work samples and your decision worksheet.',
 '<ul><li><strong>Mentorship:</strong> Who is the named supervisor, when do we work together and who covers their absence?</li><li><strong>Workload:</strong> What are the actual rota, emergency duties, travel and time-off arrangements?</li><li><strong>Responsibility:</strong> Which decisions can I make, which need review, and how do referrals or escalation work?</li><li><strong>Learning:</strong> Is there protected feedback time, training, equipment and access to the cases or methods promised?</li><li><strong>Terms:</strong> Can I see pay, deductions, contract length, notice and any bond in writing?</li></ul>'+toolkit)
 community='<p class="community-context">These are individual Reddit experiences, not verified employment statistics or eligibility guidance. We use them to identify questions worth asking; salary claims are not treated as evidence.</p>'
 for c in DATA['community']:
  community+=drawer(b,e(c['title']),e(c['context']),paras([('What the discussion raised.',e(c['observation'])),('Our practical takeaway.',e(c['action']))])+b.link(c['url'],'Read the original discussion','community-source'))
 decide+=drawer(b,'Lessons from working vets on Reddit','Three experiences, translated into better questions.',community)
 out+=panel(b,'how-to-decide','How to decide','Find a direction that fits your life—and test it before committing.',decide)
 return out
